  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain and snow showers downwind of Lake Erie gradually\ntaper off today and tonight. \n\nOngoing cold air advection (CAA) downwind of the Lower Great\nLakes via W/NW low level wind trajectories will result in\nscattered lake effect and terrain enhanced rain/snow streamers \ntoday. Vertical profiles indicate rather shallow moisture, which\nshould lead to rather brief and weak flurries/drizzle today.\nHigh resolution guidance indicates that precipitation should\ntaper off later today and tonight. \n\nIt will be another chilly day with widespread cloud cover. Brisk\nNW winds 15-25 mph will add a winter- like chill factor with \ndaytime temps feeling no better than 30-45 degrees.\n\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Another extended stretch of dry and seasonal \nweather next week.\n\nLake effect rain and snow showers come to an end by Monday \nmorning as an upper level ridge builds into the region. Clouds\nwill linger on Monday, but clear out by Tuesday as high \npressure at the surface and aloft will provide dry conditions \nwith mainly clear skies and moderating temperatures. Highs \nshould reach the 55-65F range by the second half of next week or\n+10-15 degrees above the historical average for mid November. \nLight winds, low humidity, and abundant sunshine will be ideal \nfor any outdoor activities. Whether raking leaves, putting out \nholiday decorations, or enjoying time outside, it will be an \nideal stretch of weather.\n\nWith the resumption of a very dry stretch, fire weather \nconcerns could increase as we get later into November and \nDecember. Any particularly windy patterns with minimal \nprecipitation could lead to an elevated risk of rapid wildfire \nspread. The next chance for precipitation looks like it could be\nthe end of next weekend (Sunday) at this point.",
